WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:03.810
Coronavirus isn't just a threat to your health, it's a threat to the economy.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.440
As infection numbers ticked up, the stock
market had it's worst day in 12 years.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.400
So it's probably not surprising that President Trump has promised very dramatic

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.140
plans to help keep the economy up and running, Basically, we're talking about a new stimulus.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:22.820
But that raises just a different question, can you really solve a public health problem with economic policy?

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:28.100
With me today to talk about the coronavirus economy and what to do about it is Veronqiue de Rugy.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.320
She's a senior research fellow at the Mercatus
Center at George Mason University,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.700
and a frequent contributor to Reason Magazine.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.220
Veronique, thanks for coming to talk with
me.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.420
Thanks for having me.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.920
So, isn't this the government's job

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.400
to step in times of economic crisis and help people out?

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.780
What is wrong with President Trump saying that he wants dramatic intervention measures?

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:57.640
So the problem isn't so much that the government would do something in terms of emergency,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.240
the question is what can they really do. And that's really actually, that's really the big question.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:08.740
Because this is not an emergency like we've seen, this is not the Great Recession,

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:16.040
this is not a natural disaster that keeps the whole city under water, it's very different.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:27.600
It's a public health issue with economic consequences and it's kind of unclear what the best way to go about it.

